  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "U1",
      "answer": "Interdigital tinea pedis (athlete's foot) caused by dermatophytes.",
      "rationale": "The clinical presentation—bilateral interdigital maceration, peeling, mild erythema, and exposure to communal showers in a young athlete—is classic for tinea pedis. There are no atypical features such as vesicles, bullae, or nail changes, and the patient lacks risk factors for alternative diagnoses like immunosuppression or diabetes.",
      "changed_from_previous": false
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U2",
      "answer": "Self-care is appropriate; no formal medical visit is required initially.",
      "rationale": "Given the classic presentation and absence of red-flag features, over-the-counter topical antifungals combined with hygiene measures are sufficient for initial management. The condition is not emergent, and evidence supports self-care in such cases without need for immediate outpatient evaluation.",
      "changed_from_previous": true
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U3",
      "answer": "Primary-care clinicians can manage this case; referral to dermatology is only indicated in cases of diagnostic uncertainty or treatment failure.",
      "rationale": "Uncomplicated tinea pedis is well within the scope of primary care. Dermatology referral is not necessary for definitive diagnosis or management unless the case proves refractory or atypical features emerge.",
      "changed_from_previous": true
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U4",
      "answer": "Key differential diagnoses include candidal intertrigo, contact dermatitis, erythrasma, dyshidrotic eczema, psoriasis, scabies, and bacterial intertrigo. Red-flag features suggesting a more serious condition include pain, swelling, warmth, fever, extensive erythema, vesicles or bullae, deep fissures, purulent discharge, rapid spread, immunosuppression, diabetes, or signs of secondary bacterial infection (e.g., cellulitis).",
      "rationale": "These differentials share overlapping features with tinea pedis. Red flags would suggest complications such as secondary infection or systemic involvement, necessitating more urgent evaluation and possible escalation of care.",
      "changed_from_previous": false
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U5",
      "answer": "No diagnostic tests are indicated initially; diagnosis is clinical. KOH preparation or fungal culture may be used if the case is atypical, refractory, or diagnosis is uncertain.",
      "rationale": "In typical presentations like this one, clinical diagnosis is highly accurate. Ancillary testing adds cost and delay without benefit in straightforward cases.",
      "changed_from_previous": false
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U6",
      "answer": "First-line management includes a topical antifungal (e.g., terbinafine 1% cream, clotrimazole 1%, or miconazole) applied twice daily for 2–4 weeks, along with hygiene measures: keep feet dry, change socks daily, wear breathable footwear, use antifungal powder, wear flip-flops in communal showers, and decontaminate shoes and socks.",
      "rationale": "Topical antifungals are effective for interdigital tinea pedis. Adjunctive hygiene reduces reinfection risk and supports treatment success.",
      "changed_from_previous": false
    },
    {
      "question_id": "U7",
      "answer": "Follow-up: monitor for improvement over 2 weeks; return to care if no response. Patient education: complete full antifungal course, maintain foot dryness, avoid barefoot exposure in shared facilities, launder socks in hot water, decontaminate shoes with antifungal spray, be aware of potential spread to nails or skin surfaces, and continue sports with preventive measures. Assess for secondary bacterial infection by checking for increased pain, purulent discharge, spreading erythema, or fever; if present, consider bacterial culture and antibiotics. Escalate to oral antifungals (e.g., terbinafine, itraconazole) only if topical therapy fails or infection is extensive. Footwear decontamination: apply antifungal spray daily during treatment and for 1 week after; rotate shoes to allow drying; wash socks at >60°C or with antifungal additive. Return-to-sport: no restrictions; continue activity with use of flip-flops in showers and clean, dry socks.",
      "rationale": "Comprehensive education ensures adherence and prevents recurrence. Secondary infection should be assessed clinically; escalation is rare in immunocompetent patients. Decontamination protocols reduce environmental reservoirs. Athletes can safely continue sports with precautions.",
      "changed_from_previous": false
    }
  ],
